</section>
<section id="A1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.6 </span>Quantitative Stability Analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantitatively validate our claim that PF-DAG produces more stable trajectories by reducing mode bouncing, we analyze the <span id="A1.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">total end-effector jerk</span> in our real-world experiments (Section 4.2), where stability is critical. Jerk, a standard metric for motion smoothness, is the integral of the squared magnitude of the third derivative of position over the trajectory duration <math id="A1.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Jerk}=\int_{0}^{T}\left\|\frac{d^{3}\mathbf{p}(t)}{dt^{3}}\right\|^{2}dt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Jerk</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Jerk}=\int_{0}^{T}\left\|\frac{d^{3}\mathbf{p}(t)}{dt^{3}}\right\|^{2}dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A lower total jerk indicates a physically smoother, less shaky, and more stable trajectory. We computed this metric for the contact-rich ‘Wipe Table’ task from our real-world evaluation, comparing PF-DAG against DP3. As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.6 Quantitative Stability Analysis ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Primary-Fine Decoupling for Action Generation in Robotic Imitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, PF-DAG achieves significantly lower jerk, confirming it generates smoother, less erratic end-effector movements.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T8" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T8.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T8.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Method</th>
<th id="A1.T8.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Total Jerk (<math id="A1.T8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T8.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T8.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">DP3</th>
<td id="A1.T8.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.25</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T8.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T8.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="A1.T8.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PF-DAG (Ours)</span></th>
<td id="A1.T8.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A1.T8.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.45</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Total end-effector jerk (<math id="A1.T8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) comparison on the real-world ’Wipe Table’ task.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.7 </span>Rigorous Analysis of the MSE Trade-off</h3>

<div id="A1.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis in the main text assumes an oracle <math id="A1.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to illustrate how our architecture decomposes variance. Here, we provide a more rigorous analysis of the practical trade-off, considering errors from our learned <math id="A1.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS7.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.7.1 </span>The Problem with MSE-Optimal Predictors</h4>

<div id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The central thesis of our paper is that in multi-modal tasks, a predictor that is “optimal” under the Mean Squared Error (MSE) criterion is undesirable. A standard Behavioral Cloning (BC) model that predicts the conditional expectation <math id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}^{*}(o)=\mathbb{E}[a|o]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}^{*}(o)=\mathbb{E}[a|o]</annotation></semantics></math> is, by definition, the optimal deterministic predictor. Its minimum achievable loss is:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}^{*}=\mathbb{E}_{o}[Var(a|o)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}^{*}=\mathbb{E}_{o}[Var(a|o)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Using the law of total variance, we decompose this loss:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}^{*}=\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o,m}[Var(a|o,m)]}_{V_{\text{intra}}}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o}[Var_{m|o}(\mathbb{E}[a|o,m])]}_{V_{\text{inter}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}^{*}=\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o,m}[Var(a|o,m)]}_{V_{\text{intra}}}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o}[Var_{m|o}(\mathbb{E}[a|o,m])]}_{V_{\text{inter}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{intra}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{intra}}</annotation></semantics></math>: The <span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">within-mode variance</span>. This is the fine-grained variation that our <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> must model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math>: The <span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">inter-mode variance</span>. This is the variance between the means of the different modes (<span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, the difference between “go left” and “go right”).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The MSE-optimal predictor <math id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[a|o]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[a|o]</annotation></semantics></math> averages these modes, resulting in <math id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a fundamental component of its error. This is precisely <span id="A1.SS7.SSS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mode collapse</span>, which is catastrophic for task success.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS7.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.7.2 </span>The PF-DAG Trade-Off: <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{classify}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>classify</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{classify}}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our two-stage model, PF-DAG, makes a “hard” mode selection <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{m}=\pi_{1}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{m}=\pi_{1}(o)</annotation></semantics></math>. The final action is the prediction of the second stage, <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}_{\text{PF-DAG}}(o)=\mathbb{E}_{z}[\pi_{2}(o,\hat{m},z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>PF-DAG</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}_{\text{PF-DAG}}(o)=\mathbb{E}_{z}[\pi_{2}(o,\hat{m},z)]</annotation></semantics></math>. For this analysis, let’s assume a perfect <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that correctly predicts the mean of its target mode, <span id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{z}[\pi_{2}(o,k,z)]=\mathbb{E}[a|o,m=k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{z}[\pi_{2}(o,k,z)]=\mathbb{E}[a|o,m=k]</annotation></semantics></math>, which we denote <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The practical MSE of our model is <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\mathbb{E}_{o,a}[||a-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PF-DAG</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\mathbb{E}_{o,a}[||a-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We decompose this by conditioning on the true, unobserved mode <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\mathbb{E}_{o}\left[\sum_{m}p(m|o)\mathbb{E}_{a|o,m}[||a-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2}]\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PF-DAG</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>m</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\mathbb{E}_{o}\left[\sum_{m}p(m|o)\mathbb{E}_{a|o,m}[||a-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2}]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Using the identity <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[||X-c||^{2}]=Var(X)+||\mathbb{E}[X]-c||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[||X-c||^{2}]=Var(X)+||\mathbb{E}[X]-c||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=a|o,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=a|o,m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\mathbb{E}_{o}\left[\sum_{m}p(m|o)(Var(a|o,m)+||\mu_{m}(o)-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PF-DAG</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>m</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\mathbb{E}_{o}\left[\sum_{m}p(m|o)(Var(a|o,m)+||\mu_{m}(o)-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o,m}[Var(a|o,m)]}_{V_{\text{intra}}}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o}\left[\sum_{m}p(m|o)||\mu_{m}(o)-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2}\right]}_{E_{\text{classify}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PF-DAG</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>m</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>classify</mtext></msub></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o,m}[Var(a|o,m)]}_{V_{\text{intra}}}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o}\left[\sum_{m}p(m|o)||\mu_{m}(o)-\mu_{\hat{m}(o)}(o)||^{2}\right]}_{E_{\text{classify}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This reveals the explicit trade-off of our architecture:</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-Stage (BC):</span> <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}^{*}=V_{\text{intra}}+V_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}^{*}=V_{\text{intra}}+V_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PF-DAG (Ours):</span> <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=V_{\text{intra}}+E_{\text{classify}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PF-DAG</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>classify</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PF-DAG}}=V_{\text{intra}}+E_{\text{classify}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">PF-DAG is designed to trade <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the guaranteed, catastrophic cost of mode collapse) for <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{classify}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>classify</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{classify}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the probabilistic cost of misclassification). Our strong empirical task success (Tables 1, 2, 5) supports our hypothesis that <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> is fatal for task execution, while <math id="A1.SS7.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{classify}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>classify</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{classify}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-catastrophic and manageable error. Our framework replaces a guaranteed failure mode with a high-probability success, which is a highly desirable trade-off for robotic imitation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.8 </span>Acknowledgments on LLM Usage</h3>

<div id="A1.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We acknowledge the use of a large language model (LLM) for aiding in the writing and polishing of this paper. The LLM is used as a tool to improve the clarity, grammar, and style of certain sections. Its contributions are limited to editorial and linguistic improvements, and it is not used to generate novel ideas, perform research, or formulate the core technical content. All scientific contributions, experimental results, and intellectual content are the original work of the human authors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
